WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.042 --> 00:00:00.975
[rats squeaking]

00:00:00.975 --> 00:00:04.370
Having a swarm of rats crawl up your legs

00:00:04.370 --> 00:00:07.590
may seem like your own personal nightmare.

00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:10.200
But for Hollywood animal
trainer Mark Harden,

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:12.140
it's just another day of work.

00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:14.440
To teach them to climb up his pants

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:16.970
for the 2003 movie "Willard,"

00:00:16.970 --> 00:00:19.770
multiple rats had to repeat the task

00:00:19.770 --> 00:00:22.470
over and over again at different angles.

00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:23.753
Mark Harden: At the beginning,
we certainly want to

00:00:23.753 --> 00:00:25.230
make it as easy as possible,

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:27.420
give them as much confidence as possible,

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:30.000
and then we gradually
make it more difficult.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:31.750
Narrator: But being a rat trainer

00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:34.251
isn't just about getting a rat to run

00:00:34.251 --> 00:00:35.951
from point A to point B,

00:00:35.951 --> 00:00:38.234
although that is a big part of it.

00:00:38.234 --> 00:00:40.180
It's also about understanding

00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:44.123
the unique personality
of every rat you cast.

00:00:45.070 --> 00:00:48.170
Rats are social animals
that can be trained

00:00:48.170 --> 00:00:49.980
just like a cat or a dog,

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:53.690
and they have a similar
genetic makeup to humans.

00:00:53.690 --> 00:00:55.700
And just like humans, they can have

00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:58.690
a variety of behaviors and personalities,

00:00:58.690 --> 00:01:00.950
which means rat trainers need to be able

00:01:00.950 --> 00:01:02.270
to sort through them

00:01:02.270 --> 00:01:05.650
and identify their
strengths and weaknesses.

00:01:05.650 --> 00:01:08.440
For each main rat character in a film,

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:10.210
Mark actually needs to find

00:01:10.210 --> 00:01:13.080
multiple rat actors to play the part.

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:16.050
In "Willard," Mark used six different rats

00:01:16.050 --> 00:01:18.860
to play the main white rat, Socrates,

00:01:18.860 --> 00:01:20.920
who was on camera most of the film

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:23.600
doing a variety of specialized tricks.

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:24.970
Mark: This guy's a really good runner.

00:01:24.970 --> 00:01:27.310
This guy seems pretty
handleable and stable,

00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:29.260
so if the actors have to hold him,

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:30.450
this will be my actor rat.

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:33.780
So you sort of slot them into
the arc of the character.

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:36.430
Narrator: A horde of rats
is called a mischief.

00:01:36.430 --> 00:01:38.810
And once Mark has cast the mischief,

00:01:38.810 --> 00:01:42.640
the rats need to be sorted
by what they can and will do.

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:44.560
Mark: Henry Ford would be very proud of

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:47.340
how we train rats in the groups.

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:49.460
Narrator: And he has
trained a lot of rats,

00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:51.340
even for a single project.

00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:53.990
300 for "Bram Stoker's Dracula,"

00:01:53.990 --> 00:01:56.189
and 600 for "Willard."

00:01:56.189 --> 00:02:00.200
After casting, the first step
is to split them up by gender,

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:02.560
so they don't reproduce while filming.

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:05.350
Then he splits them into smaller groups

00:02:05.350 --> 00:02:08.700
and puts them into boxes
based on their assigned task,

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:10.660
which is based on what they're good at,

00:02:10.660 --> 00:02:12.830
according to the trainer's observation.

00:02:12.830 --> 00:02:15.320
Each box might have a different trainer

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:16.960
or trainers in charge of it.

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:19.910
They might start out
with five rats in a box,

00:02:19.910 --> 00:02:22.168
and eventually they
would work their way up

00:02:22.168 --> 00:02:23.550
to 20 at a time.

00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:25.433
To keep track of who is who,

00:02:25.433 --> 00:02:27.666
all rats are tagged on their tail

00:02:27.666 --> 00:02:29.210
with a different color marker.

00:02:29.210 --> 00:02:30.608
Mark: It was a team effort.

00:02:30.608 --> 00:02:33.766
I didn't sit there and have, you know,

00:02:33.766 --> 00:02:35.060
600 rats on a leash.

00:02:35.060 --> 00:02:37.637
Narrator: One of the types
of rats he wants to identify

00:02:37.637 --> 00:02:40.260
early on are the more aggressive rats,

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:42.620
or biters, for safety reasons.

00:02:42.620 --> 00:02:44.720
Yes, rats do bite people,

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:46.920
and Mark himself has been bitten.

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:48.690
But in general, they won't go

00:02:48.690 --> 00:02:50.720
out of their way to attack someone.

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:52.970
Mark: So, we encourage
biting certain things.

00:02:52.970 --> 00:02:55.180
But what you would hand to an actor

00:02:55.180 --> 00:02:59.110
would be one that you're
100% sure is not a biter.

00:02:59.110 --> 00:03:01.380
Narrator: But let's say
he does want the rat

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:03.000
to bite something on camera.

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:05.860
In this case, a rat that likes to chew

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:07.940
is exactly what he'd want.

00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:11.090
The trick is putting food near the object,

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:14.320
then eventually putting
the food inside the object.

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:17.650
A biter rat will chew its
way through to get to it,

00:03:17.650 --> 00:03:20.060
just like they do in
this scene from "Willard"

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:21.143
with the newspaper

00:03:21.143 --> 00:03:23.270
and this scene with the tires.

00:03:23.270 --> 00:03:26.170
Mark creates a special
mixture of nut butter

00:03:26.170 --> 00:03:29.170
and food coloring, which
he paints on a prop.

00:03:29.170 --> 00:03:31.910
For example, in this scene in "Willard,"

00:03:31.910 --> 00:03:35.680
the rats were actually
chewing fake balsa-wood legs

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:37.740
covered in this edible paste.

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:39.970
Mark: If I want him to look
like he's biting a finger,

00:03:39.970 --> 00:03:41.900
I'll just put some scent on the finger.

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:44.040
Not enough to make him want to taste it,

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:45.640
but enough to make him interested.

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:48.920
Narrator: If a real bite is
required close up on camera,

00:03:48.920 --> 00:03:51.078
usually a prosthetic is used,

00:03:51.078 --> 00:03:53.036
with fake blood of course.

00:03:53.036 --> 00:03:56.680
No matter what the rat's
personality or specialty is,

00:03:56.680 --> 00:03:59.260
they all have one trait in common.

00:03:59.260 --> 00:04:00.930
Mark: The problem with
rats is they're very

00:04:00.930 --> 00:04:03.330
sound sensitive and smell sensitive,

00:04:03.330 --> 00:04:05.510
and they're very tactile.

00:04:05.510 --> 00:04:09.080
So when you're training
them, as soon as you move,

00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:10.950
it's all different, right?

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:12.810
The first smell that wafts through

00:04:12.810 --> 00:04:15.930
that they don't recognize, they shut down.

00:04:15.930 --> 00:04:18.010
Narrator: So the more details about a set

00:04:18.010 --> 00:04:19.950
he knows in advance, the better,

00:04:19.950 --> 00:04:22.270
so he can prepare them for anything.

00:04:22.270 --> 00:04:24.670
Let's look at this scene in "True Blood"

00:04:24.670 --> 00:04:27.030
that Mark worked on as an example.

00:04:27.030 --> 00:04:29.720
A rat needed to run on
dirt through a tunnel

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:31.990
while a camera was pushing towards him.

00:04:31.990 --> 00:04:35.730
It then had to crawl through
a grate and run to a mark,

00:04:35.730 --> 00:04:38.460
where it would then stand on its hind legs

00:04:38.460 --> 00:04:40.650
and transform into a human.

00:04:40.650 --> 00:04:44.150
So Mark needed to first
identify a good runner rat

00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:46.070
who could deal with these obstacles.

00:04:46.070 --> 00:04:47.920
He replicated what it would look like

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:50.450
as much as possible in his training area,

00:04:50.450 --> 00:04:54.430
using a simple dog toy on a
paint stick to mimic the camera.

00:04:54.430 --> 00:04:57.960
And how did he get the rat
to move in the first place?

00:04:57.960 --> 00:05:00.360
No matter the personality, he has to use

00:05:00.360 --> 00:05:03.170
several basic behavior-modification tools

00:05:03.170 --> 00:05:06.110
to get it to do what he wants on command.

00:05:06.110 --> 00:05:08.400
First is positive reinforcement,

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:10.810
in which he rewards the rats with food

00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:12.590
after the trick is complete.

00:05:12.590 --> 00:05:14.980
Or he might reward them with safety

00:05:14.980 --> 00:05:17.400
by giving them what's called a sanctuary.

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:19.490
Mark: We have little plastic shoeboxes

00:05:19.490 --> 00:05:21.190
with a little mousehole cut in it.

00:05:21.190 --> 00:05:23.090
They're dark, they're black.

00:05:23.090 --> 00:05:25.120
They're very attractive to a rat.

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:26.470
Their food's in there.

00:05:26.470 --> 00:05:29.310
So we start introducing
them to the concept of food

00:05:29.310 --> 00:05:31.320
and sanctuary at the same time.

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:34.740
Narrator: But food alone isn't
going to get a rat to run.

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:37.372
Often, Mark also has to take advantage

00:05:37.372 --> 00:05:40.630
of its flight reaction
by making a loud sound.

00:05:40.630 --> 00:05:42.450
Mark: A rat is built to deal with stress.

00:05:42.450 --> 00:05:43.970
If you don't give them any stress,

00:05:43.970 --> 00:05:47.470
they tend to do self-destructive
behaviors anyway.

00:05:47.470 --> 00:05:50.290
So, their whole psychology is built from

00:05:50.290 --> 00:05:52.300
scurrying from this thing to that thing.

00:05:52.300 --> 00:05:53.920
Narrator: For the "True Blood" scene,

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:56.590
Mark made a makeshift rattle with pebbles

00:05:56.590 --> 00:05:58.165
to get the rat to run

00:05:58.165 --> 00:06:00.856
and rewarded it with
a sanctuary at the end

00:06:00.856 --> 00:06:02.380
where it would get food.

00:06:02.380 --> 00:06:04.827
Mark: And then we start
increasing the distance,

00:06:04.827 --> 00:06:06.243
but we add a buzzer to it.

00:06:06.243 --> 00:06:07.685
[buzzer sounding]
So we're calling them

00:06:07.685 --> 00:06:10.434
and giving them some
aversion to being here

00:06:10.434 --> 00:06:11.840
while we're calling them there.

00:06:11.840 --> 00:06:13.154
So by the time you get on set,

00:06:13.154 --> 00:06:15.150
you usually, just the buzzer is enough.

00:06:15.150 --> 00:06:17.190
But if he goes sideways or something,

00:06:17.190 --> 00:06:20.600
you might wanna shake your little stick.

00:06:20.600 --> 00:06:22.960
Narrator: To get the rat
to hit its final mark,

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:26.680
he used an almond-oil scent
so it knew where to stop.

00:06:26.680 --> 00:06:29.140
He uses this same basic method

00:06:29.140 --> 00:06:31.531
when he needs specialized tricks,

00:06:31.531 --> 00:06:33.340
like jumping or climbing.

00:06:33.340 --> 00:06:35.420
Mark: They behave well on props.

00:06:35.420 --> 00:06:37.118
They can walk tightropes and go up ladders

00:06:37.118 --> 00:06:38.190
and do things like that.

00:06:38.190 --> 00:06:40.690
Narrator: Let's say he wants
a rat to jump in a scene,

00:06:40.690 --> 00:06:42.950
a more difficult trick for a rat.

00:06:42.950 --> 00:06:45.670
He puts the food behind him on a platform,

00:06:45.670 --> 00:06:49.170
so the rat has to use his
shoulder as a bridge to get it.

00:06:49.170 --> 00:06:52.010
Then he gradually increases the distance,

00:06:52.010 --> 00:06:53.750
so it has to hop over.

00:06:53.750 --> 00:06:56.370
Or if he wants it to climb his leg,

00:06:56.370 --> 00:06:57.731
as we mentioned earlier,

00:06:57.731 --> 00:07:00.755
he puts the food at
waist level off camera,

00:07:00.755 --> 00:07:03.670
so it needs to go up his leg to get to it.

00:07:03.670 --> 00:07:06.540
And sometimes he needs to adjust the props

00:07:06.540 --> 00:07:08.480
to give the rats a boost.

00:07:08.480 --> 00:07:10.346
For example, in "Willard,"

00:07:10.346 --> 00:07:12.521
they created a set of prop steps

00:07:12.521 --> 00:07:14.880
that the rats could easily run down

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:16.718
that were much shorter than the ones

00:07:16.718 --> 00:07:18.380
Crispin Glover would use.

00:07:18.380 --> 00:07:21.210
Now, working with a
single rat is one thing,

00:07:21.210 --> 00:07:22.770
but what happens when he needs

00:07:22.770 --> 00:07:24.780
a whole group to do something?

00:07:24.780 --> 00:07:27.120
How do their personalities change?

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:29.230
Mark: The groups become individuals.

00:07:29.230 --> 00:07:32.150
I mean, if you have a group of 300 rats,

00:07:32.150 --> 00:07:35.250
you break it down into manageable pieces,

00:07:35.250 --> 00:07:38.040
and those pieces each
become an individual.

00:07:38.040 --> 00:07:40.930
Narrator: And each rat
needs to learn the trick.

00:07:40.930 --> 00:07:43.730
They won't follow the
herd or follow a leader,

00:07:43.730 --> 00:07:46.110
like pigeons or other flock animals.

00:07:46.110 --> 00:07:48.150
Mark: As groups, they're all stupid.

00:07:48.150 --> 00:07:52.180
One rat will never lead
another group of rats

00:07:52.180 --> 00:07:53.870
through something difficult,

00:07:53.870 --> 00:07:57.250
but one rat will stop
an entire army of rats.

00:07:57.250 --> 00:07:59.688
Narrator: A common scene
with rats in movies

00:07:59.688 --> 00:08:01.763
is swarms of them running together

00:08:01.763 --> 00:08:03.350
from one point to another.

00:08:03.350 --> 00:08:05.335
Let's look at an example of how a group

00:08:05.335 --> 00:08:07.920
of some runners were
trained for "Willard."

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:11.060
Mark starts by putting
each box one at a time

00:08:11.060 --> 00:08:13.570
on top of a table in the middle of a room.

00:08:13.570 --> 00:08:15.480
He opens it and has them run to

00:08:15.480 --> 00:08:18.250
a different sanctuary
box at the other end.

00:08:18.250 --> 00:08:19.770
He does this on a table,

00:08:19.770 --> 00:08:21.990
both to get them out of their comfort zone

00:08:21.990 --> 00:08:24.080
and so they're easier to control.

00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:26.290
Mark: They naturally want to hug a wall.

00:08:26.290 --> 00:08:28.640
So, I'm in the moviemaking business,

00:08:28.640 --> 00:08:31.500
so anything they wanna do
naturally we train against,

00:08:31.500 --> 00:08:34.300
because chances are that's not
what you want in the movie.

00:08:34.300 --> 00:08:36.500
Narrator: He will
gradually lower the table

00:08:36.500 --> 00:08:38.800
until the rats learn to run on the floor.

00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:40.908
Mark: And then we start introducing them

00:08:40.908 --> 00:08:43.108
to multiple surfaces.

00:08:43.108 --> 00:08:44.820
They'll run over carpet,
they'll run over wood,

00:08:44.820 --> 00:08:45.850
they'll run over tile.

00:08:45.850 --> 00:08:48.000
We'll put water, we'll put dirt,

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:49.540
we'll put whatever they need.

00:08:49.540 --> 00:08:52.190
Narrator: Lastly, he
preps the groups together

00:08:52.190 --> 00:08:53.582
on the actual set.

00:08:53.582 --> 00:08:56.570
Usually, two rehearsals is enough.

00:08:56.570 --> 00:08:59.440
In groups, rats all need to be weighed

00:08:59.440 --> 00:09:02.170
two to three times per day to make sure

00:09:02.170 --> 00:09:04.270
they are all eating and healthy.

00:09:04.270 --> 00:09:06.430
Faster, smarter rats in the group

00:09:06.430 --> 00:09:08.820
will get to the food first more often,

00:09:08.820 --> 00:09:11.340
so Mark occasionally needs to identify

00:09:11.340 --> 00:09:15.220
and remove them to let the
other rats get the prize.

00:09:15.220 --> 00:09:18.810
So a lot of time on set
is spent just making sure

00:09:18.810 --> 00:09:21.490
the animals are all safe and healthy.

00:09:21.490 --> 00:09:24.040
And just how strong is
their group identity?

00:09:24.040 --> 00:09:25.340
Mark: We would prep the blue team,

00:09:25.340 --> 00:09:27.360
then we would prep the red team,

00:09:27.360 --> 00:09:28.751
and then we'd turn them all loose

00:09:28.751 --> 00:09:30.793
and see what they would do.

00:09:30.793 --> 00:09:31.750
And we kept track,

00:09:31.750 --> 00:09:36.740
and about 85% of the rats
ended up in their own boxes.

00:09:36.740 --> 00:09:39.430
Narrator: Many films these days use CGI

00:09:39.430 --> 00:09:42.210
to create realistic animals like rats.

00:09:42.210 --> 00:09:45.700
But with CGI, you're also
losing the personality

00:09:45.700 --> 00:09:47.510
that comes with the real thing.

00:09:47.510 --> 00:09:50.780
Mark: It's really less
dynamic and less dramatic.

00:09:50.780 --> 00:09:52.718
Narrator: And you just
wouldn't get that same

00:09:52.718 --> 00:09:56.200
visceral reaction watching
a bunch of CGI rats

00:09:56.200 --> 00:10:00.080
crawl up a human leg as you
would with a real swarm.

00:10:00.080 --> 00:10:02.970
Mark: On one time in
the rehearsal, the rat,

00:10:02.970 --> 00:10:05.470
one rat got inside my pant leg.

00:10:05.470 --> 00:10:07.650
And that just freaked me out,

00:10:07.650 --> 00:10:09.550
because you got 100 rats on the outside

00:10:09.550 --> 00:10:10.848
and one on the inside.

00:10:10.848 --> 00:10:12.960
And it was the freakiest feeling.

